A frequency map keeps the current group of trees easy to manage. The right side adds fruits, and the left side moves forward whenever three fruit types appear.

For `fruits = [1, 2, 3, 2, 2]`, the window becomes invalid at `3`. Removing from the left gives `[2, 3, 2, 2]`, which has length 4.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    int totalFruits(vector<int>& fruits){
        unordered_map<int, int> count;
        int left = 0;
        int best = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < fruits.size(); right++) {
            count[fruits[right]]++;

            while (count.size() > 2) {
                int fruit = fruits[left];
                count[fruit]--;
                if (count[fruit] == 0) {
                    count.erase(fruit);
                }
                left++;
            }

            best = max(best, right - left + 1);
        }

        return best;
    }
};
```

An empty input returns 0 because the loop is never entered. The input array is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` here, because the map holds at most three types while shrinking.

When fruit values are limited to a small known range, an array is simpler than a hash map. The array stores how many of each type are inside the current window, and `types` tells how many non-empty fruit types there are.

The code below assumes the usual fruit value range from the problem, where a vector sized by the largest value is safe. It does not change the input array.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    int totalFruits(vector<int>& fruits){
        if (fruits.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int largest = 0;
        for (int fruit : fruits) {
            largest = max(largest, fruit);
        }

        vector<int> frequency(largest + 1, 0);
        int left = 0;
        int types = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < fruits.size(); right++) {
            if (frequency[fruits[right]] == 0) {
                types++;
            }
            frequency[fruits[right]]++;

            while (types > 2) {
                frequency[fruits[left]]--;
                if (frequency[fruits[left]] == 0) {
                    types--;
                }
                left++;
            }

            answer = max(answer, right - left + 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

For a single-element array, the first window has length 1. The frequency array uses space based on the largest fruit value, so a hash map is better if values can be very large.

Time complexity: `O(n + M)`, where `M` is the largest fruit value.
Space complexity: `O(M)`.

This is another constant-space version. The variables describe the two fruit types currently usable, while `lastCount` remembers the consecutive block at the end of the window. When a third type appears, that block can stay with the new fruit, so the window length resets to `lastCount + 1`.

For `[1, 1, 2, 2, 3]`, the best window before `3` is length 4. After `3`, only the ending block of `2`s can be carried into the new window.

```go
func totalFruits(fruits []int) int {
	lastType := -1
	previousType := -1
	lastCount := 0
	currentLength := 0
	best := 0

	for _, fruit := range fruits {
		if fruit == lastType || fruit == previousType {
			currentLength++
		} else {
			currentLength = lastCount + 1
		}

		if fruit == lastType {
			lastCount++
		} else {
			previousType = lastType
			lastType = fruit
			lastCount = 1
		}

		if currentLength > best {
			best = currentLength
		}
	}

	return best
}
```

The empty slice returns 0, and a slice containing one fruit type returns its full length. No input values are changed.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Instead of storing every count, this version stores the last position of each fruit type. When a third type arrives, the type with the oldest last position is removed. Moving the left pointer just after that position leaves the largest valid window ending at the current index.

This map can briefly contain three entries, then one is removed immediately.

```java
class Solution {
    public int totalFruits(int[] fruits) {
        java.util.HashMap<Integer, Integer> lastSeen = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        int left = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < fruits.length; right++) {
            lastSeen.put(fruits[right], right);

            if (lastSeen.size() > 2) {
                int oldestFruit = fruits[right];
                int oldestIndex = right;

                for (java.util.Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : lastSeen.entrySet()) {
                    if (entry.getValue() < oldestIndex) {
                        oldestFruit = entry.getKey();
                        oldestIndex = entry.getValue();
                    }
                }

                lastSeen.remove(oldestFruit);
                left = oldestIndex + 1;
            }

            answer = Math.max(answer, right - left + 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

If all trees have one type, the whole array is accepted. The array itself stays unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since the map has at most three entries when searching for the oldest type.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

